The spec revision is posted as "[PATCH] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec
the list's mail hub" [1]. It rewrites jails/instances/lab-mail/spec.org
in place for the hub, with Dovecot as the only writer. The notes below
its "---" list each decision from this thread, and the four the spec
makes that the thread did not. Those four are where to look first:

- mbsync's own Dovecot login needs a password, kept beside
  mail-creds. Every jail on beastie can reach 127.0.0.1:2143, so the
  login that may write the list must have a secret.
- The well-formedness check runs in OpenSMTPD's session, before it
  answers DATA. A check at delivery would come after the 250, and its
  bounce would go to an address the server discards.
- Your channel from beastie uses Expunge Both, and the agents' uses
  Expunge Near. With Expunge None there, an archived message would
  stay in lists, marked deleted, on lab-mail and on every client.
  lab-mail to Fastmail stays Expunge None, so Fastmail keeps every
  message and a mistaken deletion can be undone from there.
- pat@p7x.dev is a known address beside pat@patmaddox.com, since your
  client on beastie sends as pat@p7x.dev.

Here are the two scenarios. First, the concern: three writers, each as
its own user, on today's file permissions. mbsync owns the maildir,
its folders are 0700, and its files 0600.

1. 10:00:00. Fastmail receives your post M. lab-mail's mbsync pulls it
   into lists/new as a 0600 file owned by mbsync.
2. 10:00:05. The agent posts a reply R. OpenSMTPD tries to write it
   into lists/new as its delivery user. That folder is mbsync's and
   0700, so the delivery fails, and R waits in OpenSMTPD's queue.
3. 10:00:30. You archive M from beastie. Your mbsync asks Dovecot to
   move M from lists to Archive. Dovecot runs as vmail. The move is a
   rename out of lists/cur, which needs write on a folder that mbsync
   owns 0700. The rename fails with EACCES, Dovecot answers NO, and
   your mbsync logs an error on every run until someone fixes it.

The fix within that design is a shared group with 0770 folders, and a
job that widens what mbsync creates after every run. Even then:

4. 10:01:00. The Fastmail archive folder appears for the first time.
   mbsync creates Archive 0700. Until the widening job runs, Dovecot
   cannot enter it, and your move fails again.
5. Dovecot's user can now unlink every file. An agent being read-only
   rests on Dovecot's ACL alone. That part does not depend on the
   design, as the last point below says.

I need to correct one thing I said last time. I wrote that a file in
the gap before the chmod is one Dovecot cannot move. Moving it needs
write on the folder, not on the file. What a 0600 file blocks is
reading it, so a client fetch fails in that gap. The folder is the
real obstacle, and it still blocks moves as above.

Second, the fixed version. Dovecot owns the maildir and is the only
program that opens it. mbsync and OpenSMTPD are clients of Dovecot.

1. 10:00:00. Fastmail receives M. lab-mail's mbsync logs in to
   Dovecot as mbsync and appends M to lists. Dovecot writes the file,
   assigns its UID, and tells IDLE clients at once.
2. 10:00:05. The agent posts R. OpenSMTPD hands it to Dovecot over
   LMTP, and Dovecot writes it the same way. OpenSMTPD never opens the
   maildir.
3. 10:00:30. You archive M. Your mbsync asks Dovecot to move it.
   Dovecot owns both folders, so the rename succeeds.
4. 10:01:00. lab-mail's mbsync runs. Over IMAP it sees R as new in
   lists and M as gone from lists and new in Archive. It appends R to
   Fastmail's lists and M to Fastmail's Archive, and marks M deleted
   in Fastmail's lists.
5. An agent logged in as claude@p7x.dev tries to expunge R. Dovecot's
   ACL refuses it.

No step depends on file modes, a group, or a chmod job, and no file
changes behind Dovecot's back. The cost is in step 5. File permissions
no longer back up the ACL, because vmail can write every file. The same
is true with three writers, once your login can delete.

